  "summary": "Several significant healthcare infrastructure and investment trends emerged today. AI adoption continues to accelerate across clinical settings, but healthcare systems are struggling with fragmented data and workflow integration challenges—experts warn that AI ambitions are outpacing infrastructure readiness. Major consolidation activity includes Johnson & Johnson's $785 million cell therapy partnership with Sail Bio, Argenx's $2 billion acquisition of Forte Bio for autoimmune treatments, and Included Health acquiring Firefly Health to expand employer health plan offerings, alongside smaller deals like Doctronic acquiring Summer Health for pediatric care. On the regulatory front, the Trump administration is revising its 340B drug discount program rebate pilot, drawing hospital backlash, while the FTC has embroiled Hims & Hers in another controversy. New clinical advances include FDA clearance for Samsung's smart ring for sleep apnea detection and an FDA advisory panel's recommendation for Replimune's melanoma treatment after two prior rejections, though Novo Nordisk's inflammation-targeting heart disease drug missed its study endpoints. Funding rounds reflected continued investor confidence in health tech, with Function Health securing $450 million in growth financing and startups ranging from smart toilet sensors to AI revenue cycle management platforms raising tens of millions.",
